Mechanism: the BFS in TrustedCertificates.checkPathValidity/reachesValidEnd links certificates by subject/issuer and signature only (no basicConstraints/keyUsage/pathLen), so an off-path presented certificate that is valid by date but not a valid issuer can stand in for the expired given anchor the platform actually used. Fixture added to lpFixtures(): lpCrossByCaNotCa = keytool -gencert -alias ca -infile cross.csr (no -ext bc:c, so getBasicConstraints()==-1); lpCrossExpiredSelfPem = keytool -gencert -alias cross -keystore cross.p12 -infile cross.csr -ext bc:c -startdate 2000/01/01 -validity 2 (expired, same subject and key as the cross intermediate, which signed crossLeaf).

